    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is currently no scientific consensus that ancestral populations developed distinct, innate cognitive speeds or temperaments solely due to environmental selective pressures. Research consistently shows that while genetic variation exists within and between populations, observable differences in \"processing speed\" or \"temperament\" are primarily driven by individual experiences, socioeconomic factors, and test bias rather than deep-seated biological programming. Scholars agree that human cognitive abilities are plastic and adaptable, meaning that any perceived group differences have minimal predictive power regarding individual potential or intelligence.",
